/*  The Cufet lexer, written in Cufet.

        cufet tools/lexer/lexer.cufe <file.cufe> [more…]

    Prints every token of every file named, one per line:

        file    <the path, as given>
        <token type> <line> <column> <lexeme>
        …
        Eof     <line> <column>

    separated by tabs, with a backslash, a line break, a tab and a carriage return in a lexeme
    written `\\`, `\n`, `\t` and `\r` so that a token is always one line. A file the lexer refuses
    prints `error`, the line, the column and the reason instead of its tokens — exactly what
    `src/Lexer/Lexer.cs` does, which throws before handing any token over.

    ★★ The answer key is `src/Lexer/Lexer.cs` itself. `LexerParityTests` runs this over every
    `.cufe` file in the repository and compares it with `new Lexer(source).Tokenize()`, token by
    token, so this file and that one cannot drift apart without a test saying where.

    ⚠ Columns count CHARACTERS here and UTF-16 units there. They agree everywhere except after a
    character outside the Basic Multilingual Plane on the same line, and the test converts.
*/

Pull a book on collections.
    Define object scanner with (
        the chase source,
        the number size,
        the number cursor,
        the number row,
        the number row-start,
        the series of text out,
        the map from text to number kinds,
        the map from text to text marks,
        the map from text to text words).

    // ── Where the scan stands ─────────────────────────────────────────────

    /// The character under the scan, or "" once the source is used up.
    Bind text to peek unto scanner:
        If one's cursor is greater than one's size, return "".
        Return item (one's cursor) of one's source.
    Done.

    /// The character after the one under the scan, or "" past the end.
    Bind text to ahead unto scanner:
        If one's cursor + 1 is greater than one's size, return "".
        Return item (one's cursor + 1) of one's source.
    Done.

    Bind void to advance unto scanner:
        Increment one's cursor by 1.
    Done.

    Bind fact to at-end unto scanner:
        Return one's cursor is greater than one's size.
    Done.

    /// The 1-based column of the character at `spot`, which must sit on the current line.
    Bind number to column-at unto scanner, given (the number spot):
        Return spot - one's row-start + 1.
    Done.

    // ── What a character is ───────────────────────────────────────────────

    /// ⚠ Cufet cannot ask what a character IS. A table answers for ASCII, and outside it a
    /// character with two cases is a letter. That matches `char.IsLetter` on every character in
    /// this repository, and not on an uncased script — which none of it is written in.
    Bind fact to is-letter unto scanner, given (the text ch):
        Define kind as (the entry for ch in one's kinds) but void is 0.
        If kind is 1, return true.
        If kind is not 0, return false.
        If ch is "", return false.
        Return (ch in lowercase) is not (ch in uppercase).
    Done.

    Bind fact to is-digit unto scanner, given (the text ch):
        Return ((the entry for ch in one's kinds) but void is 0) is 2.
    Done.

    Bind fact to is-space unto scanner, given (the text ch):
        Return ((the entry for ch in one's kinds) but void is 0) is 3.
    Done.

    Bind fact to is-word-part unto scanner, given (the text ch):
        If cast one's is-digit on (ch), return true.
        Return cast one's is-letter on (ch).
    Done.

    /// A lowercase letter is one whose uppercase is something else.
    Bind fact to is-lower, given (the text ch):
        Return (ch in uppercase) is not ch.
    Done.

    // ── What the scan produces ────────────────────────────────────────────

    Bind void to emit unto scanner, given (the text kind, the text lexeme, the number at-row, the number at-column):
        Insert "{kind}\t{at-row}\t{at-column}\t{cast escaped on (lexeme)}" into one's out.
    Done.

    /// A refusal, in the shape the failure carries it: line, column and reason, tab-separated.
    Bind text to refusal, given (the number at-row, the number at-column, the text reason):
        Return "{at-row}\t{at-column}\t{reason}".
    Done.

    /// A lexeme on one line: the four characters that could break one written as escapes.
    Bind text to escaped, given (the text raw):
        Define result as replace "\\" with "\\\\" in raw.
        The result becomes replace "\n" with "\\n" in result.
        The result becomes replace "\t" with "\\t" in result.
        The result becomes replace "\r" with "\\r" in result.
        Return result.
    Done.

    // ── Between tokens ────────────────────────────────────────────────────

    /// A line break inside a literal, consumed and counted. A CRLF is ONE break, as it is in
    /// Lexer.cs — see NewlineInLiteral there for why that is a language rule.
    Bind fact to newline-in-literal unto scanner:
        Define ch as cast one's peek.
        If ch is "\r":
            If cast one's ahead is not "\n", return false.
            Cast one's advance.
        Done.
        Otherwise if ch is not "\n":
            Return false.
        Done.
        Increment one's row by 1.
        Cast one's advance.
        One's row-start becomes one's cursor.
        Return true.
    Done.

    Bind void or failure to skip-space unto scanner:
        While cast one's at-end is false, repeat:
            Define ch as cast one's peek.
            Define next-ch as cast one's ahead.
            If ch is "\n":
                Increment one's row by 1.
                One's row-start becomes one's cursor + 1.
                Cast one's advance.
            Done.
            Otherwise if cast one's is-space on (ch):
                Cast one's advance.
            Done.
            Otherwise if ch is "/" and next-ch is "/":
                Cast one's skip-line-comment.
            Done.
            Otherwise if ch is "/" and next-ch is "*":
                Cast one's skip-block-comment or pass the failure off.
            Done.
            Otherwise:
                Return.
            Done.
        Done.
    Done.

    /// Everything to the end of the line. The line break is left for skip-space, so the row is
    /// counted in one place.
    Bind void to skip-line-comment unto scanner:
        While cast one's at-end is false, repeat:
            If cast one's peek is "\n", stop.
            Cast one's advance.
        Done.
    Done.

    /// A `/* … */`, NESTED: an inner opener needs its own closer. Unterminated is refused at the
    /// OUTERMOST opener, which is the one a writer has to go and find.
    Bind void or failure to skip-block-comment unto scanner:
        Define opened-row as one's row.
        Define opened-column as cast one's column-at on (one's cursor).
        Define depth as 1.
        Cast one's advance.
        Cast one's advance.
        // `/**/` is an empty ordinary comment, not a doc comment nobody closed.
        If cast one's peek is "*" and cast one's ahead is not "/", cast one's advance.
        While depth is greater than 0, repeat:
            If cast one's at-end:
                Return a failure (cast refusal on (opened-row, opened-column,
                    "unterminated comment — expected '*/' to close it")).
            Done.
            Define ch as cast one's peek.
            Define next-ch as cast one's ahead.
            If ch is "\n":
                Increment one's row by 1.
                One's row-start becomes one's cursor + 1.
                Cast one's advance.
            Done.
            Otherwise if ch is "/" and next-ch is "*":
                Increment depth by 1.
                Cast one's advance.
                Cast one's advance.
            Done.
            Otherwise if ch is "*" and next-ch is "/":
                Decrement depth by 1.
                Cast one's advance.
                Cast one's advance.
            Done.
            Otherwise:
                Cast one's advance.
            Done.
        Done.
    Done.

    // ── One token ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

    Bind void or failure to read-one unto scanner:
        Define ch as cast one's peek.
        Define next-ch as cast one's ahead.
        If cast one's is-letter on (ch):
            Cast one's read-word or pass the failure off.
        Done.
        Otherwise if cast one's is-digit on (ch):
            Cast one's read-number or pass the failure off.
        Done.
        Otherwise if ch is "\"":
            Cast one's read-string or pass the failure off.
        Done.
        // Before the symbols, which would take the first '<' as Lt.
        Otherwise if ch is "<" and next-ch is "<":
            Cast one's read-raw or pass the failure off.
        Done.
        Otherwise if ch is "[":
            Cast one's read-axiom or pass the failure off.
        Done.
        Otherwise if (the entry for ch in one's marks) is not void:
            Cast one's read-symbol.
        Done.
        Otherwise if ch is "'":
            Cast one's read-possessive or pass the failure off.
        Done.
        Otherwise if ch is ".":
            Cast one's emit on ("Dot", ".", one's row, cast one's column-at on (one's cursor)).
            Cast one's advance.
        Done.
        Otherwise:
            Return a failure (cast refusal on (one's row, cast one's column-at on (one's cursor),
                "unexpected character '{ch}'")).
        Done.
    Done.

    Bind void or failure to read-word unto scanner:
        Define begun as one's cursor.
        Define piece as a chase.
        While cast one's at-end is false, repeat:
            Define ch as cast one's peek.
            If cast one's is-word-part on (ch):
                Insert ch into piece.
                Cast one's advance.
            Done.
            // A dash only when a letter or digit follows it — internal, never trailing.
            Otherwise if ch is "-" and cast one's is-word-part on (cast one's ahead):
                Insert ch into piece.
                Cast one's advance.
            Done.
            Otherwise:
                Stop.
            Done.
        Done.

        Define lexeme as piece converted to text.
        Define lowered as lexeme in lowercase.
        Define kind as (the entry for lowered in one's words) but void is "Identifier".
        Define column as cast one's column-at on (begun).
        Define lead as the first 1 characters of lexeme.

        If kind is "Identifier" and cast is-lower on (lead) is false:
            // Contextual statement words may open a statement with a capital.
            If lowered is "output" or lowered is "seed" or lowered is "make":
                Cast one's emit on (kind, lexeme, one's row, column).
                Return.
            Done.
            If lexeme is "Add":
                Return a failure (cast refusal on (one's row, column,
                    "'Add' is not a statement any more — it became 'Insert … into'. "
                    joined to "Write 'Insert 4 into scores.' where you would have written 'Add 4 to scores.' before")).
            Done.
            Return a failure (cast refusal on (one's row, column,
                "identifier '{lexeme}' must start with a lowercase letter")).
        Done.

        Cast one's emit on (kind, lexeme, one's row, column).
    Done.

    Bind void or failure to read-number unto scanner:
        Define prefix as (cast one's ahead) in lowercase.
        If cast one's peek is "0" and (prefix is "x" or prefix is "b" or prefix is "o"):
            Cast one's read-bits or pass the failure off.
            Return.
        Done.

        Define begun as one's cursor.
        Define piece as a chase.
        While cast one's is-digit on (cast one's peek), repeat:
            Insert cast one's peek into piece.
            Cast one's advance.
        Done.
        // A '.' is a decimal point only when a digit follows it; otherwise it ends the statement.
        If cast one's peek is "." and cast one's is-digit on (cast one's ahead):
            Insert "." into piece.
            Cast one's advance.
            While cast one's is-digit on (cast one's peek), repeat:
                Insert cast one's peek into piece.
                Cast one's advance.
            Done.
        Done.
        Cast one's emit on ("Number", piece converted to text, one's row, cast one's column-at on (begun)).
    Done.

    Bind fact to is-digit-of-base, given (the text ch, the text base):
        If ch is "", return false.
        If base is "x", return "0123456789abcdefABCDEF" contains ch.
        If base is "o", return "01234567" contains ch.
        Return "01" contains ch.
    Done.

    Bind text to base-name, given (the text base):
        If base is "x", return "hex".
        If base is "o", return "octal".
        Return "binary".
    Done.

    /// 0x, 0b and 0o. `_` groups digits and is dropped, and the digit count sets the width.
    Bind void or failure to read-bits unto scanner:
        Define opened-row as one's row.
        Define opened-column as cast one's column-at on (one's cursor).
        Cast one's advance.
        Define written as cast one's peek.
        Define base as written in lowercase.
        Cast one's advance.

        Define digits as a chase.
        While cast one's at-end is false, repeat:
            Define ch as cast one's peek.
            If ch is "_":
                // Only BETWEEN digits: a leading, trailing or doubled one is a typo.
                If the number of digits is 0 or cast is-digit-of-base on (cast one's ahead, base) is false:
                    Return a failure (cast refusal on (opened-row, opened-column,
                        "'_' must sit between digits in a 0{written} literal")).
                Done.
                Cast one's advance.
                Skip.
            Done.
            If cast is-digit-of-base on (ch, base) is false, stop.
            Insert ch into digits.
            Cast one's advance.
        Done.

        If the number of digits is 0:
            Return a failure (cast refusal on (opened-row, opened-column,
                "'0{written}' needs at least one {cast base-name on (base)} digit after it")).
        Done.
        If cast one's at-end is false and cast one's is-word-part on (cast one's peek):
            Return a failure (cast refusal on (opened-row, opened-column,
                "'{cast one's peek}' is not a {cast base-name on (base)} digit")).
        Done.

        Define per-digit as 4 when base is "x", otherwise (3 when base is "o", otherwise 1).
        Define width as the number of digits * per-digit.
        If width is greater than 64:
            Return a failure (cast refusal on (opened-row, opened-column,
                "this literal is {width} bits wide, and bits values hold at most 64 — "
                joined to "64 bits covers every C flag set and address, so anything wider belongs in a "
                joined to "library reached through the foreign function interface")).
        Done.

        Cast one's emit on ("Bits", "0{base}{digits converted to text}", opened-row, opened-column).
    Done.

    Bind void to read-symbol unto scanner:
        Define column as cast one's column-at on (one's cursor).
        Define ch as cast one's peek.
        Define kind as (the entry for ch in one's marks) but void is "".
        Cast one's advance.
        If cast one's peek is "=" and (ch is "<" or ch is ">"):
            Cast one's advance.
            Define wide as "Lte" when ch is "<", otherwise "Gte".
            Cast one's emit on (wide, ch joined to "=", one's row, column).
            Return.
        Done.
        Cast one's emit on (kind, ch, one's row, column).
    Done.

    Bind void or failure to read-possessive unto scanner:
        Define column as cast one's column-at on (one's cursor).
        Cast one's advance.
        If cast one's peek is "s":
            Cast one's advance.
            Cast one's emit on ("Possessive", "'s", one's row, column).
            Return.
        Done.
        Return a failure (cast refusal on (one's row, column, "unexpected character '''")).
    Done.

    // ── Text ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

    /// A quoted literal. Plain, it is one String token at the opening quote. With a hole in it,
    /// it is InterpolOpen, then pieces and holes, then InterpolClose — and ⚠ a piece, a hole's
    /// braces and the closer each report where the scan STANDS when it is emitted, not where it
    /// began, because that is what Lexer.cs does.
    Bind void or failure to read-string unto scanner:
        Define opened-row as one's row.
        Define opened-column as cast one's column-at on (one's cursor).
        // The opener goes in front of everything the holes produce, so where it goes is kept.
        Define mark as the number of one's out.
        Cast one's advance.
        Define piece as a chase.
        Define holed as false.

        Define scanning as true.
        While scanning, repeat:
            If cast one's at-end:
                Return a failure (cast refusal on (one's row, cast one's column-at on (one's cursor),
                    "unterminated string literal")).
            Done.
            Define ch as cast one's peek.

            If ch is "\"":
                Cast one's advance.
                The scanning becomes false.
                Skip.
            Done.

            If ch is "\\":
                Cast one's advance.
                If cast one's at-end:
                    Return a failure (cast refusal on (one's row, cast one's column-at on (one's cursor),
                        "unterminated string literal")).
                Done.
                Define escape as cast one's peek.
                Cast one's advance.
                If escape is "n":
                    Insert "\n" into piece.
                Done.
                Otherwise if escape is "t":
                    Insert "\t" into piece.
                Done.
                Otherwise if escape is "r":
                    Insert "\r" into piece.
                Done.
                Otherwise if escape is "\\" or escape is "\"" or escape is "\{" or escape is "\}":
                    Insert escape into piece.
                Done.
                Otherwise:
                    Return a failure (cast refusal on (one's row, cast one's column-at on (one's cursor - 1),
                        "unrecognized escape sequence '\\{escape}'")).
                Done.
                Skip.
            Done.

            If ch is "\{":
                The holed becomes true.
                If the number of piece is greater than 0:
                    Cast one's emit on ("StringPiece", piece converted to text, one's row,
                        cast one's column-at on (one's cursor)).
                    The piece becomes a chase.
                Done.
                Cast one's advance.
                Cast one's emit on ("InterpolHoleOpen", "\{", one's row, cast one's column-at on (one's cursor - 1)).

                Cast one's skip-space or pass the failure off.
                If cast one's at-end or cast one's peek is "\}":
                    Return a failure (cast refusal on (one's row, cast one's column-at on (one's cursor),
                        "empty interpolation — write an expression between the braces")).
                Done.

                // Brace depth, so an object literal inside a hole does not close it.
                Define depth as 1.
                While depth is greater than 0, repeat:
                    If cast one's at-end:
                        Return a failure (cast refusal on (one's row, cast one's column-at on (one's cursor),
                            "unterminated interpolation")).
                    Done.
                    Cast one's skip-space or pass the failure off.
                    If cast one's at-end:
                        Return a failure (cast refusal on (one's row, cast one's column-at on (one's cursor),
                            "unterminated interpolation")).
                    Done.
                    Define inner as cast one's peek.
                    If inner is "\{":
                        Increment depth by 1.
                        Cast one's advance.
                        Cast one's emit on ("LBrace", "\{", one's row, cast one's column-at on (one's cursor - 1)).
                    Done.
                    Otherwise if inner is "\}":
                        Decrement depth by 1.
                        Cast one's advance.
                        Define closer as "InterpolHoleClose" when depth is 0, otherwise "RBrace".
                        Cast one's emit on (closer, "\}", one's row, cast one's column-at on (one's cursor - 1)).
                    Done.
                    Otherwise:
                        // Anything else — words, numbers, a string with holes of its own.
                        Cast one's read-one or pass the failure off.
                    Done.
                Done.
                Skip.
            Done.

            If cast one's newline-in-literal:
                Insert "\n" into piece.
                Skip.
            Done.
            Cast one's advance.
            Insert ch into piece.
        Done.

        If holed is false:
            Cast one's emit on ("String", piece converted to text, opened-row, opened-column).
            Return.
        Done.

        If the number of piece is greater than 0:
            Cast one's emit on ("StringPiece", piece converted to text, one's row,
                cast one's column-at on (one's cursor)).
        Done.
        Define opener as "InterpolOpen\t{opened-row}\t{opened-column}\t".
        If mark is 0, insert opener into the start of one's out.
        Otherwise, insert opener after item mark of one's out.
        Cast one's emit on ("InterpolClose", "", one's row, cast one's column-at on (one's cursor)).
    Done.

    /// `<< … >>` — nothing inside is interpreted, and the pairs nest.
    Bind void or failure to read-raw unto scanner:
        Define opened-row as one's row.
        Define opened-column as cast one's column-at on (one's cursor).
        Cast one's advance.
        Cast one's advance.
        Define piece as a chase.
        Define depth as 1.
        While depth is greater than 0, repeat:
            If cast one's at-end:
                Return a failure (cast refusal on (opened-row, opened-column,
                    "unterminated verbatim text — expected '>>' to close it")).
            Done.
            Define ch as cast one's peek.
            Define next-ch as cast one's ahead.
            If ch is "<" and next-ch is "<":
                Increment depth by 1.
                Cast one's advance.
                Cast one's advance.
                Insert "<<" into piece.
            Done.
            Otherwise if ch is ">" and next-ch is ">":
                Cast one's advance.
                Cast one's advance.
                Decrement depth by 1.
                If depth is greater than 0, insert ">>" into piece.
            Done.
            Otherwise if cast one's newline-in-literal:
                Insert "\n" into piece.
            Done.
            Otherwise:
                Cast one's advance.
                Insert ch into piece.
            Done.
        Done.
        Cast one's emit on ("String", piece converted to text, opened-row, opened-column).
    Done.

    /// `[ … ]` — foreign source, kept as written. Bracket pairs nest, and a backslash escapes the
    /// next character for COUNTING only: both are kept.
    Bind void or failure to read-axiom unto scanner:
        Define opened-row as one's row.
        Define opened-column as cast one's column-at on (one's cursor).
        Cast one's advance.
        Define piece as a chase.
        Define depth as 1.
        While depth is greater than 0, repeat:
            If cast one's at-end:
                Return a failure (cast refusal on (opened-row, opened-column,
                    "unterminated foreign source — expected ']' to close it")).
            Done.
            Define ch as cast one's peek.
            If ch is "\\" and cast one's ahead is not "":
                Cast one's advance.
                Insert "\\" into piece.
                If cast one's newline-in-literal:
                    Insert "\n" into piece.
                Done.
                Otherwise:
                    Insert cast one's peek into piece.
                    Cast one's advance.
                Done.
            Done.
            Otherwise if ch is "[":
                Increment depth by 1.
                Cast one's advance.
                Insert "[" into piece.
            Done.
            Otherwise if ch is "]":
                Cast one's advance.
                Decrement depth by 1.
                If depth is greater than 0, insert "]" into piece.
            Done.
            Otherwise if cast one's newline-in-literal:
                Insert "\n" into piece.
            Done.
            Otherwise:
                Cast one's advance.
                Insert ch into piece.
            Done.
        Done.
        Cast one's emit on ("Axiom", piece converted to text, opened-row, opened-column).
    Done.

    // ── A whole file ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────

    Bind void or failure to tokenize unto scanner:
        While cast one's at-end is false, repeat:
            Cast one's skip-space or pass the failure off.
            If cast one's at-end, stop.
            Cast one's read-one or pass the failure off.
        Done.
        Cast one's emit on ("Eof", "", one's row, cast one's column-at on (one's cursor)).
    Done.

    // ── The tables ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

    /// 1 a letter, 2 a digit, 3 a space. The spaces are every character `char.IsWhiteSpace`
    /// answers yes to — most of which no text literal can spell, so they go in by code point.
    Define kinds as a map from text to number.
    Define letters as a chase.
    Insert "abcdefghijklmnopqrstuvwxyzABCDEFGHIJKLMNOPQRSTUVWXYZ" into letters.
    For each ch in letters, repeat:
        In kinds, the entry for ch becomes 1.
    Done.
    Define numerals as a chase.
    Insert "0123456789" into numerals.
    For each ch in numerals, repeat:
        In kinds, the entry for ch becomes 2.
    Done.
    Define spaces as a chase.
    For each code in a series with (9, 10, 11, 12, 13, 32, 133, 160, 5760, 8192, 8193, 8194, 8195,
            8196, 8197, 8198, 8199, 8200, 8201, 8202, 8232, 8233, 8239, 8287, 12288), repeat:
        Insert code into spaces.
    Done.
    For each ch in spaces, repeat:
        In kinds, the entry for ch becomes 3.
    Done.

    Define marks as a map with (
        "+" : "Plus", "-" : "Minus", "*" : "Star", "/" : "Slash", "%" : "Percent",
        "(" : "LParen", ")" : "RParen", "=" : "Equal", ":" : "Colon", "," : "Comma",
        "\{" : "LBrace", "\}" : "RBrace", "|" : "Pipe", "<" : "Lt", ">" : "Gt").
    Define words as cast keyword-table.

    For each name in the arguments, repeat:
        State "file\t{name}".
        Try to:
            Define whole as read all from the file name.
            Define buffer as a chase.
            Insert whole into buffer.
            Define scan as a new scanner {
                the source buffer, the size the number of buffer, the cursor 1,
                the row 1, the row-start 1, the out a series of text,
                the kinds kinds, the marks marks, the words words }.
            Cast scan's tokenize.
            For each token in scan's out, repeat:
                State token.
            Done.
        Done.
        In case of failure:
            State "error\t{the message of the failure}".
        Done.
    Done.
Done.
